WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.299 --> 00:00:09.450
The White House has been the residence of
every president since the second, John Adams.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.380
But though it's today the center of national
attention, that hasn't always been the case.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:19.140
During most of the 19th century, the country
was far more interested in what was happening

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.660
in Congress on Capitol Hill than what was
happening here at the White House.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:29.720
But we live today in a presidential age, so
much so that many Americans think that the

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.540
president is in charge of National Affairs.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.510
That's a perception that the political scientist
Hugh Hecle calls "the illusion of presidential

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:38.510
government."

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:44.450
Now, to be sure, the presidency today is far
more powerful than it was in the 19th century.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:49.670
Government is much larger, and the president
oversees a large number of executive agencies.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.290
As well, the United States is a major player
on the world stage.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:55.489
That brings the presidency to the forefront.

00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:59.980
Yet the reality is, the presidency operates
in a system of divided powers.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.699
The presidency is one branch of the national
government.

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:09.220
The president can propose legislation but
unless Congress goes along, there is no law.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.290
That's the reality of the American system.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:17.120
Theodore Roosevelt, one of the great 20th
century presidents, said it best.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.470
"I wish, if only for a day, to be Congress
as well as the president.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.570
Then," said Roosevelt, "I'd truly be in charge
of the national government."

